{"gid":"2026_04_ARI_NYG","week":4,"away":"ARI","home":"NYG","away_col":"#E64868","home_col":"#5C8FF0","spread":7.0,"total":45.5,"fav":"NYG","hpts":26,"apts":19,"kickoff":"Sun Oct 4 \u00b7 13:00","roof":"Outdoors","report_html":"<div class=\"report\"><p><strong>Script leans NYG.</strong> As a 7-point favorite they project to lead much of the game, tilting toward the run late \u2014 <strong>Cam Skattebo</strong>'s carry ceiling rises in the blowout branches. Meanwhile ARI throw to keep pace, a volume tailwind for <strong>Michael Wilson</strong>.</p><p>The <strong>NYG</strong> host the <strong>ARI</strong> as favorites of <strong>7.0</strong>, with the total set at <strong>45.5</strong> \u2014 a market that implies a <strong>NYG 26\u201319</strong> scoreline. Running the game forward from that baseline, we expect a contest that spends roughly <strong>38% of its snaps with NYG in front</strong>, <strong>38% within one score</strong>, and <strong>24% with NYG chasing</strong>. That distribution is the engine of everything below: game state is what turns a team's tendencies into a specific player's workload, so before we get to individuals it's worth being explicit about <em>how</em> this game is likely to be played.</p><p>This projects as a game <strong>NYG should control</strong>. A favorite of 7 sits in front for the majority of the second half in most simulations, which pulls the script toward the run and clock management for <strong>NYG</strong> and toward volume passing and tempo for <strong>ARI</strong>, who will be throwing to keep pace. Expect <strong>ARI</strong> to run more total plays than NYG \u2014 trailing teams go no-huddle (league no-huddle rate climbs from ~8% in neutral game states to over 20% when down two scores), and that extra volume is a tailwind for ARI's pass-catchers even in a losing effort.</p><h3>The Key Matchups</h3><p><strong>Cam Skattebo</strong> eats as a receiver too \u2014 ARI give up 23 rec yds/game to backs, and NYG target the RB on 18% of throws.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>Isaiah Likely</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 ARI are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.32 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><p><strong>Malik Nabers</strong> carries TD equity \u2014 ARI surrender 0.47 WR1 receiving scores a game.</p><p>In the red zone ARI throw it (59% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Michael Wilson</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p>A middling 46 total \u2014 no strong lean either way; the value is in the individual matchups above, not the game environment.</p><h3>NYG \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>NYG are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (42% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p><strong>Cam Skattebo</strong> (14 carries, 59 yards projected) gets a mild game-script tailwind as a favorite, though NYG's willingness to keep throwing when ahead caps how much the lead converts into extra carries.</p><p><strong>Malik Nabers</strong> projects for <strong>11 targets and 83 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Isaiah Likely</strong> (TE, 7 targets, 60 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>ARI \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>ARI are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (45% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 ARI project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>Tyler Allgeier</strong>'s rushing projection (15 carries, 62 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Michael Wilson</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, ARI funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>10 targets for 83 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Trey McBride</strong> (TE, 13 targets, 98 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 7-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the \u25c6-flagged rows below) are where soft lines and real edge tend to appear \u2014 those depth players get fed in blowouts, and the market is slowest to price them.</p><p><em>This preview and the projections below regenerate through the week as injuries, weather and line moves update the inputs.</em></p></div>","teams":[{"team":"ARI","col":"#E64868","players":[{"name":"Jacoby Brissett","pos":"QB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Pass yards","proj":259.21,"book":null,"over_th":245.16,"under_th":271.84,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"pover":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null,"skew":false},{"label":"Pass TDs","proj":1.42,"book":null,"over_th":1.0,"under_th":1.39,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"pover":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null,"skew":false}],"td":{"pct":9.0,"fair":"12/1","book":null,"value":null},"d":{"pass_yards":{"m":259.21,"q":[109.94,191.07,258.68,324.51,391.23